-Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} shows the DGF architecture. The two $A/E$ channels share encoder weights. The time--frequency branch represents a complex Morlet CWT by its modulus and argument. The Morlet parameter is $\mu=13.4$. We evaluate scales $1,2,\ldots,16$ using reflection padding and $L^1$ wavelet normalization, and retain every fourth time sample, giving a $2\times16\times264$ array per channel. Mixing along the temporal, feature and scale axes, followed by convolutional embedding and linear projection, produces $8\times32\times64$ features.
-
-A second Morlet CWT uses scales $\mu f_s/(2\pi f_r)$, with 16 nominal frequencies $f_r$ logarithmically spaced from $0.25$ to $0.002\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and the same padding and normalization. Both CWTs use the observed channel divided by its RMS, floored at $10^{-8}$. Three convolutional stages with group normalization and GELU map these low-frequency features to an additive correction before the time--frequency branch projection.
+Figure~\ref{fig:architecture} shows the DGF architecture. The two $A/E$ channels share encoder weights. The time--frequency branch represents a complex Morlet CWT by its modulus and argument. The Morlet parameter is $\mu=13.4$. The first bank uses scales $a=1,2,\ldots,16$, with reflection padding and $L^1$ normalization. Its nominal center frequencies are $\mu f_s/(2\pi a)$: scales 5--16 peak between 0.853 and 0.267 Hz, while scales 1--4 have centers above Nyquist. Retaining every fourth time sample gives a $2\times16\times264$ feature array per channel. Mixing along the temporal, feature and scale axes, followed by convolutional embedding and linear projection, produces $8\times32\times64$ features.
+
+For the reflected 2048-sample FFT, the Morlet response at a sampled frequency $f_k$ is proportional to $\widehat\psi(2\pi a f_k/f_s)$, with the Nyquist bin halved by the implementation. The maximum discrete gains at scales 1--3 are at most $3.6\times10^{-4}$ of the continuous peak gain; scale 4 retains about 0.70 of that gain near Nyquist. This bank supplies predominantly high-frequency features. The accompanying manifest records each filter's peak frequency and gain.
+
+The low-frequency adapter uses a second Morlet CWT at scales $\mu f_s/(2\pi f_r)$, with 16 nominal frequencies $f_r$ logarithmically spaced from $0.25$ to $0.002\,\mathrm{Hz}$ and the same padding and normalization. Both CWTs use the observed channel divided by its RMS, floored at $10^{-8}$. Three convolutional stages with group normalization and GELU map these low-frequency features to an additive correction before the time--frequency branch projection.
 
 The temporal branch subtracts the observed mean, divides by the observed standard deviation and computes an eight-level db4 decomposition. Eight reconstructed subbands preserve signed time-domain information. Each is divided into 32 overlapping patches of 64 samples with stride 32, giving the same $8\times32\times64$ feature shape. The relation $1056=64+31\times32$ fixes the input length.
 
